        "We organise the analysis around a simple framework in which a firm employs high-skilled and low-skilled workers who perform tasks that differ in teleworkability. Remote work affects output through two channels. It saves commuting time and allows longer or more focused working hours, raising effective labour input [1][6]; and it reduces the frequency of informal face-to-face interaction, lowering the productivity of tasks that require coordination [23][24]. The net productivity effect is positive when the share of independent, teleworkable tasks is high and when the firm invests in digital tools that substitute for in-person communication. Because such investments take time, the productivity effect should build up gradually after adoption.",
